***********************************************************
 
          CARL CORPORATION, NATIONAL WRITERS UNION
                 TEAM UP FOR ROYALTY SYSTEM
             AT UNCOVER ARTICLE DELIVERY SERVICE
 
   CHICAGO, June 24, 1995 -- The CARL Corporation, operator 
of the UnCover database of magazine and journal articles, 
has announced an agreement in principle with the National 
Writers Union (NWU) for the establishment of the first-ever 
transaction-based writers' royalty system in the new 
electronic media.
 
   Under the agreement, the NWU will clear copyrights and 
distribute royalties to writers for fax delivery orders at 
UnCover. The largest database of its kind in the world, 
with an index freely accessible over the Internet, UnCover 
includes more than 7 million citations from 17,000 
periodicals and adds nearly 4,000 entries a day.
 
   NWU officials said they expect to begin operations by 
August 1 at the Union's newly formed Publication Rights 
Clearinghouse (PRC). Both NWU members and non-members will 
be eligible to enroll at PRC. Complete enrollment 
information, as well as a schedule of PRC's copyright and 
administrative fees, will be announced in July. 
CARL/UnCover will then begin sending PRC detailed 
accountings and payments for orders of articles licensed by 
the agency, and PRC will forward the royalties to the 
writers. PRC will operate out of the NWU's West Coast 
national office in Oakland, California.
 
   CARL president Rebecca T. Lenzini termed the agreement 
with the NWU "a natural step" for UnCover, a service 
started in 1988.
 
   "We have always been diligent about copyright fees," 
Lenzini noted. "Recently, through the Writers Union, we 
have learned that in many cases the author, rather than the 
first print publisher, owns electronic or reprint rights. 
This arrangement simply makes use of our existing 
technology to track those cases and make sure the fees 
reach the real rights holders. We are pleased to have the 
NWU as our partner in this mission of making magazine and 
journal material available speedily and economically 
throughout the world to users of online computer services, 
while affirming the principles of copyright."
 
   NWU president Jonathan Tasini said: "This agreement is 
nothing less than historic. Creators of all kinds -- 
graphic artists, videographers and photographers, as well 
as writers -- are struggling to get paid fairly in the 
emerging digital markets. We salute CARL, a visionary, 
entrepreneurial company with roots in the public library 
tradition, and we look forward to turning this model of 
copyright clearance into the standard of the electronic 
database industry."
 
   Discussions between CARL and the NWU began in 
October 1994, following the publication in The New York 
Times of an essay by Nicholson Baker, a bestselling 
author and NWU member, which outlined the controversy over 
writers' rights on the "information superhighway." In the 
article, Baker proposed as a solution the development of a 
collective licensing system similar to that which exists in 
the music industry through organizations like the American 
Society of Composers, Authors and Publishers (ASCAP). With 
today's CARL-NWU agreement for the Publication Rights 
Clearinghouse, the "writers' ASCAP" is a reality.
 
