>>          The right to accept any information from any source.

>Even knowing it is stolen material, a danger to national security or
>whatever ?

Yes. If someone mails me an atom bomb recipe, or if I read one in netnews
or find one on my web browser at the end of a link that reads "cool stuff",
I've done nothing reprehensible.

>>          The right of every person to transform in any way any
>>          information they originate or receive.

>You are signatories to things like worldwide copyright rules already. 

This is an ethical standard, not a legal prescription. It is a way to
judge laws and policies, not a law or policy itself. To the extent that
copyright laws infringe on this right, the ERR judges those laws to be
bad laws. Using the ERR as an ethical basis, EFA would oppose such laws.

>>          The right of every person to restrict access to any information
>>          they originate or receive.

>Thats a contradiction with the one above. You say I have a right to
>transform a work into something that can't be restricted (eg GPL'ing it)
>and then state that someone has a right to restrict it.

No, it isn't a contradiction. The ERR holds that you should be free to
transform this information, and that only if you attempt to retransmit
it should you become liable under Copyright laws. This is so that when
the police seize your files and demand that you decrypt them on the
basis that they MIGHT contain copyright violations, you can ethically
decline to do so.

Thus you could add a GPL to your own copy of something without anyone
fussing, but if you tried to redistribute it under that GPL you'd be in
the wrong.

>There are various other contradictions you could drive a truck through

Please raise them - I'm sure there are some that are not so easily dismissed,
and I'm only too eager to resolve them.

