The same is true of real patents.  The question is whether or
not there is greater benefit to society in causing the patent
to be disclosed rather than it remaining a trade secret and
dying with the author.

The point of the patent system, at least in the US, is to cause
information which would otherwise remain trade secret to become
public domain after some period of time in return for a period
of legislative protection.  I.12 of the US Constitution allows
such laws to exist in the US because it is felt that they promote
progress in the sciences.  Copyright law is specifically applicable
to both the arts and sciences, and was meant primarily for works
of literature (music was only added after a legal precedent went
that way).

What I see most anti-patent people claiming is that RSA would
be publically available for everyone, and that's a Cause they
get behind.

In point of fact, it probably would not have made it out of the
US at all (export restrictions, subverted by the fact of the
patent), let alone have had its algorithm disclosed.  The only
place you'd see it is in licensed products.

The amusing thing here is that as something patented, the terms
of the patent caused the algorithm to be disclosed, and now that
the patent is set to expire, it's possible to legally reverse
engineer the thing without a violation of copyright.  If it had
not been patented, it would not be legal to reverse engineer, nor
would it have been disclosed in the first place.


I agree that for software, the period of time the patent is in
place is much too long.  The time for a startup to market for a
software product is much, much shorter than for a physically
existant object.  The value in the market place is dependent on
the terms under which the techniques employed become public as
seperate from the code.

Now consider a copyright/patent hybrid, an s-patent if you will,
and entirely new construct.

An s-patent is like a patent in that it affords protection for
disclosed information, the period of such protection being
proportional to the innovation/market curve in the industry:
I'd say two years would be a ballpark figure to start thinking
about.

The requirement is that the "publisher" disclose the algorithm
and the source code.  The algorithm disclosure is necessary to
avoid the proliferation of private languages for the source code,
rendering it useless.

The disclosure is not public; it is to an escrow agency.  The
agency then sits on the information for two years (or whatever
period) and then releases it into the public domain.  This allows
the innovators a two year jump on the market in front of their
non-innovative competition; at the same time, it does not allow
them to sit on their laurels; they must continue innovating to
have at least a product that isn't free anyway at the end of the
period.

At the same time, you disallow all normal copyright, trademark
("look-n-feel" is an issue of trademark and trade dress), and
normal patent protection for all software.

This leaves trade secret protection, but the force of that is
minimal; one must sell/license software to make money, and such
protection only lasts until the first disclosure.  If the legal
entity doing the disclosing doesn't have deep pockets, well then
you've just screwed yourself.

As a side effect, we get documented code.  8-).

I would certainly be all for some type of mechanism similar
to an s-patent.  It would certainly be more in line with the
intent of I.12 as applied to software than either the copyright
or patent law is today.  Remember that you no longer have to file
for copyright protection, nor do you have to disclose source to
obtain either a copyright or a patent.  The result is that the
material is nearly unusable, even if both the patent and the
copyright had expired (anyone know any 50-year dead programmers?).

