>Ah.  In this case, I'm probably not interested in it at all,
>since I choose to live under the US constitution.  

Perhaps I've been unclear - although the ERR is intended to apply to non-
US situations, there's no reason why it might not be applied within a US
context as well. I didn't mean to imply that your comments were not valuable
within this context.

As to patents, the issue is simply one of abstraction. If you allow people
to intellectually protect abstract things, that limits the range of real
tools that can be developed to benefit people. This effect is much more
dramatic with algorithmic patents than with regular patents for three reasons.

First, the range of tools that can incorporate an algorithm is much broader
than the range of tools that can incorporate a physical mechanism, especially
when that algorithm is stated in abstract terms. So the limitation that is 
imposed is much more restrictive.

Second, as you mentioned, algorithmic tools are developed much more
rapidly than physical tools, so the limitation that is imposed is much
more costly. An entire industry can have developed before a patent is
ratified - as almost happened with Comptom's multimedia. That one is a
limiting case, but can you imagine the impact in terms of lost jobs and
consumer opportunities if it had been upheld?

Third, with a physical patent the patent is a single program - it
describes exactly what physical components need to be in place in order
to constitute the patented technique. With an algorithmic patent the
patent is a set of programs that may contain hundreds, or even
thousands of consumable permutations. So the limitation that is imposed
is of a scope that can't be determined when the patent is evaluated.
This one is obviously underscored heavily by the RSA situation.

