This is ignorant of the history of RSA.  The technique was published
before patents were applied for (this rules out getting patents in
countries other than the US right off the bat, hence there are no
international RSA patents even for all-hardware applications).  The
authors were academics, with plenty of incentive to publish.  It would
have become available and widely known in the absence of a patent system.
(The outcome might have been different in other cases, but not this one.

You seem to believe that people know how to implement RSA because they
read the patent application.  Nonsense; such things are written in
patentese and are often almost incomprehensible and useless to engineers
and programmers (hence the notions that patents disclose inventions has
become increasingly laughable).  You can often understand the discription
of the implementation, but the claims are always much, much broader than
the implementation.  Read many patents, Terry?  I have.  People know how
to implement RSA because they read the original paper.

>The amusing thing here is that as something patented, the terms
>of the patent caused the algorithm to be disclosed, and now that
>the patent is set to expire, it's possible to legally reverse
>engineer the thing without a violation of copyright.  If it had
>not been patented, it would not be legal to reverse engineer, nor
>would it have been disclosed in the first place.

If it had not been patented, the technique would be in the public domain,
since it was published by academics before anyone considered getting a
patent.  If it had not been discovered by R, S, and A, it would have been
discovered by someone else: the key invention was the Diffie-Hellman work,
showing what you could do if you had *any* one-way function (a function
where computing the inverse is much, much harder than computing the
forward direction).  Factoring of prime numbers is a fairly obvious
candidate for a place to look for such functions.  Add a bit of
350-year-old number theory and you're there.  Many related algorithms that
will do the job have been discovered, but the RSA patent was worded
broadly enough to cover many algorithms that R, S, and A had not
themselves discovered or conceived.  The Diffie-Hellman patent claims to
cover *any* possible public key scheme.
