scoile@osf1.gmu.edu (Steve "Stevers!" Coile) writes:
>The key words in his question are "even knowing".  If you click on a
>link called "cool stuff" and receive an "atom bomb recipe", you are not
>necessarily knowingly receiving "stolen" material or material that is
>"a danger to national security or whatever".

If it were illegal to *knowingly* pull down this material then two problems
arise. First, anyone can claim that they thought it was a joke, or that it
wasn't sensitive material - for example, if I click on a link marked 
"Fat Man bomb design" I might expect to receive just the details that
have been cleared for public consumption. I won't really know until I read
what comes down.

Second, the suspicion that I may have "knowingly" pulled this stuff down
is a motivation for the law to force me to decrypt my files, register
my encryption keys and surrender my equipment for inspection. These things
are subject to terrible abuses that the ERR is intended to oppose.

I should also point out that "stolen" material is not "stolen" in the
conventional sense - you've not denied the owner the use of it, but you've
betrayed a confidence/copyright by distributing it to someone whose not
entitled to it. The ERR considers this a crime on the part of the 
distributor, not on the part of the receiver, because that's the only
way to keep the law from considering that it has that obligation to inspect
everyone's files on a regular basis. 

